```{ojs}
//| echo: false
import {palette} from "./widgets/irt.js"
import {diffusionTrials, bins, satCurves, diffusionIRT} from "./widgets/rt-process-models.js"
import {quiz, predict} from "./widgets/quiz.js"
```

## What this is for

[Response time and the speed-accuracy tradeoff](response-time.qmd) modelled time and accuracy separately, joined by a correlation, and then found in four tables that a response's time said something about whether it was right. Where does that dependence come from? A process model answers by describing the decision itself: evidence builds up, noisily, until there is enough to act on, and that one process produces both the choice and the time. This lesson builds the **diffusion model** from a random walk, finds the 2PL inside it, and fits it to three IRW tables: a lexical decision task where its simple form roughly holds, a motion task whose errors are slower than it allows, and a licensure exam whose items it wasn't built for.

## Goals

By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

- describe the diffusion model's four parameters and simulate trials from it;
- predict what a harder stimulus and a speed instruction do, and estimate the parameters with EZ-diffusion;
- derive the 2PL from the diffusion model and say what discrimination means as a process;
- explain why errors can be slower than correct responses, and why that has to be checked within a condition;
- say when a diffusion model, or a race model, suits the data in hand.

## Core ideas

### A decision as a random walk

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [Response time and the speed-accuracy tradeoff](response-time.qmd): the hierarchical model assumes **conditional independence**. Given ability, speed and the item parameters, a response's time says nothing more about whether it was right. In all four tables the conditional accuracy function said otherwise, in a different shape each time.
:::

A respondent is deciding whether a string of letters is a word. Keep a running total of the evidence, $x$, that starts at $z$ and moves upward on average when the string is a word. When it reaches an upper boundary at $\alpha$ the respondent says "word"; when it falls to 0 they say "nonword". The time to get there plus a **non-decision time** $T_{er}$, for seeing the string and pressing the key, is the response time. Formally, over each small interval $dt$ the total moves by $v\,dt$ plus normal noise with SD $s\sqrt{dt}$. This is the diffusion model ([Ratcliff, 1978](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.85.2.59); [Ratcliff & McKoon, 2008](https://doi.org/10.1162/neco.2008.12-06-420)), with four parameters:

- the **drift** $v$: the quality of the evidence, how fast the total moves toward the correct boundary;
- the **boundary separation** $\alpha$: how much evidence the respondent requires, their caution;
- the **starting point** $z$: a bias toward one response ($z = \alpha/2$ is unbiased);
- the **non-decision time** $T_{er}$.

The noise $s$ only sets the scale ([Ratcliff & McKoon, 2008](https://doi.org/10.1162/neco.2008.12-06-420)). We fix $s = 1$; the documentation of the R package `rtdists` ([Singmann et al., 2014](https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.rtdists)) notes that Ratcliff and others often use $s = 0.1$, which makes $v$ and $\alpha$ ten times smaller. The diffusion literature calls the boundary $a$; we write $\alpha$ to keep $a$ for the item slope, though the two turn out to be the same thing.

#### Try it: the random walk

Each thin line is one trial (blue ends correct, orange in an error). Below, times for 1,500 trials: correct responses above the axis, errors below.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof rw_v = Inputs.range([-1, 4], {value: 1.5, step: 0.1, label: "Drift v"})
viewof rw_a = Inputs.range([0.4, 3], {value: 1.2, step: 0.1, label: "Boundary separation α"})
viewof rw_z = Inputs.range([0.2, 0.8], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "Starting point (share of α)"})
viewof rw_t = Inputs.range([0.1, 0.6], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Non-decision time (s)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
rw = diffusionTrials(rw_v, rw_a, rw_z, rw_t)
rw_max = Math.max(1.5, 1.1 * rw.rows.map(d => d.rt).sort((x, y) => x - y)[Math.floor(0.99 * rw.rows.length)])
rw_cb = bins(rw.rows.filter(d => d.resp === 1).map(d => d.rt), 0, rw_max, 50)
rw_eb = bins(rw.rows.filter(d => d.resp === 0).map(d => d.rt), 0, rw_max, 50)
html`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.5rem">${
  Plot.plot({width: 700, height: 240, x: {label: "Seconds", domain: [0, rw_max]}, y: {label: "Evidence", domain: [-0.05 * rw_a, 1.05 * rw_a]},
    marks: [Plot.ruleY([0, rw_a], {stroke: palette.ink}),
            Plot.ruleX([rw_t], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
            Plot.line(rw.paths.filter(d => d.t <= rw_max), {x: "t", y: "x", z: "trial", stroke: d => d.resp === 1 ? palette.main : palette.contrast, strokeWidth: 1})]})
}${
  Plot.plot({width: 700, height: 200, x: {label: "Seconds", domain: [0, rw_max]}, y: {label: "Trials (errors below)"},
    marks: [Plot.rectY(rw_cb, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: "count", fill: palette.main}),
            Plot.rectY(rw_eb, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: d => -d.count, fill: palette.contrast}),
            Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: palette.rule})]})
}</div>`
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Accuracy **${rw.acc.toFixed(2)}**. Median time: correct **${rw.medCorrect.toFixed(2)} s**, errors **${isNaN(rw.medError) ? "none" : rw.medError.toFixed(2) + " s"}**.`
```

Both histograms have the long right tail that the lognormal model assumed, here produced by the walk. With the start halfway, the two medians agree to within sampling noise (0.53 and 0.55 s as the widget loads). Move the starting point to 0.3 and errors become common and fast: a walk that starts near the error boundary gets there quickly.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "With an unbiased start ($z = \\alpha/2$) and the same drift on every trial, correct responses and errors have the same distribution of times. Why?",
  options: [
    {text: "Mirroring a path about the midpoint turns a correct response into an error at the same time, and the two paths' probabilities differ by a factor that doesn't depend on the time", correct: true, why: "For a walk with drift, the ratio of a path's probability to its mirror image's depends only on where the path ends. So conditioning on the boundary reached leaves the same distribution of times."},
    {text: "Errors are rare, so their times are noisy and happen to match", correct: false, why: "The equality is exact in the model, whatever the accuracy. The widget's small differences are sampling noise."},
    {text: "The non-decision time is added to both", correct: false, why: "$T_{er}$ shifts both distributions by the same amount, but it can't make the decision times equal. The symmetry of the walk does that."}
  ]
})
```

So the simple model assumes conditional independence, trial by trial. [Kang, De Boeck and Ratcliff (2022)](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-021-09819-5) show that letting drift vary across persons and items, and the starting point vary too, produces the positive, negative and curved conditional accuracy functions found in test data, shapes like the inverted U in the response-time lesson's lexical task.

### What the parameters mean, and EZ-diffusion

With an unbiased start and $s = 1$ the model gives two closed forms:
$$\Pr(\text{correct}) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\alpha v}}, \qquad \text{mean decision time} = \frac{\alpha}{2v}\,\tanh\!\left(\frac{\alpha v}{2}\right).$$
A harder stimulus lowers the drift, so the respondent is less accurate *and* slower. An instruction to hurry lowers the boundary, so they are faster but less accurate. The response-time lesson's speed–accuracy tradeoff becomes one parameter, $\alpha$, and moving along it leaves the drift alone. [Ratcliff and McKoon (2008)](https://doi.org/10.1162/neco.2008.12-06-420) review experiments in which stimulus difficulty moved the drift and speed or accuracy instructions moved the boundaries.

#### Try it: drift or boundary?

Accuracy against mean response time ($T_{er}$ = 0.3 s) as the boundary changes (blue) or the drift changes (orange); the dot is the current setting.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof sa_v = Inputs.range([0.2, 4], {value: 1.5, step: 0.1, label: "Drift v"})
viewof sa_a = Inputs.range([0.3, 3], {value: 1.2, step: 0.1, label: "Boundary separation α"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
sa = satCurves(sa_v, sa_a)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 320, x: {label: "Mean response time (s)", domain: [0.3, 1.6]}, y: {label: "P(correct)", domain: [0.5, 1]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["change the boundary", "change the drift"], range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [Plot.line(sa.curves.filter(d => d.rt <= 1.6), {x: "rt", y: "p", stroke: "which", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
          Plot.dot([sa.point], {x: "rt", y: "p", r: 6, fill: palette.ink})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Accuracy **${sa.point.p.toFixed(3)}**, mean response time **${sa.point.rt.toFixed(2)} s**.`
```

Lower α and the dot slides down the blue curve, toward faster and less accurate responses. Lower the drift and it slides down the orange curve, toward slower *and* less accurate ones. The two leave different marks on time and accuracy together, which is how a model tells them apart.

The closed forms can be run backwards. **EZ-diffusion** ([Wagenmakers, van der Maas & Grasman, 2007](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03194023)) takes three numbers from a set of trials (the proportion correct and the mean and variance of the correct response times) and returns $v$, $\alpha$ and $T_{er}$ in closed form. It assumes the simple model (an unbiased start, no variability across trials) and ignores the error times. [Ratcliff (2008)](https://doi.org/10.3758/PBR.15.6.1218) shows that it is sensitive to outlying times and can mislead when its assumptions don't hold, and suggests it for exploration rather than final estimates. EZ is a reasonable choice for a first look at many respondents with many trials each, and it is a few lines of base R; full fits are in *Going further*.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Go deeper: the EZ equations

**Assumptions:** the diffusion model with $s = 1$, an unbiased start $z = \alpha/2$, and no across-trial variability in drift, start or non-decision time; one condition, with proportion correct $P_c$ (not 0.5 or 1) and mean $M$ and variance $V$ of the correct response times.

Accuracy gives the product: $L = \text{logit}(P_c) = \alpha v$. The variance of the decision time (which is also the variance of the response time, since $T_{er}$ is constant) is a second function of $\alpha$ and $v$; eliminating $\alpha$ gives
$$v = \text{sign}\!\left(P_c - \tfrac12\right)\left[\frac{L\left(P_c^2 L - P_c L + P_c - \tfrac12\right)}{V}\right]^{1/4}, \qquad \alpha = \frac{L}{v}.$$
The mean decision time then follows from the closed form in the main text, and $T_{er} = M - \frac{\alpha}{2v}\,\frac{1 - e^{-\alpha v}}{1 + e^{-\alpha v}}$ ([Wagenmakers et al., 2007](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03194023)). With $s = 0.1$, multiply $v$ and $\alpha$ by 0.1.

Two edge cases need care. At $P_c = 1$ the logit is infinite; the code replaces $P_c$ with $1 - 1/(2n)$ for $n$ trials, counting half an error. At $P_c = 0.5$ the drift is zero and $\alpha$ is undefined. The Simulate section checks the equations by recovery: data generated with known parameters give them back.
:::

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "In a lexical decision experiment, the same respondents are told on some blocks to respond as fast as they can. Accuracy falls from 0.92 to 0.85 and responses get faster. Under the diffusion model, which parameter changed?",
  options: [
    {text: "The boundary separation $\\alpha$", correct: true, why: "An instruction changes how much evidence the respondent waits for, not the quality of the evidence. A lower $\\alpha$ gives faster, less accurate responses, as observed."},
    {text: "The drift $v$", correct: false, why: "The strings haven't changed, so the evidence they carry hasn't either. And a lower drift would make responses slower, not faster."},
    {text: "The non-decision time $T_{er}$", correct: false, why: "A shorter $T_{er}$ speeds every response but leaves accuracy alone. It can't explain the drop from 0.92 to 0.85."}
  ]
})
```

### The diffusion model is an item response model

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [From the 1PL to the 4PL](1pl-to-4pl.qmd): the 2PL is $\Pr(x_{ij} = 1) = \text{logit}^{-1}\big(a_i(\theta_j - b_i)\big)$, where the slope or discrimination $a_i$ says how quickly the probability rises as $\theta$ passes $b_i$.
:::

Now let the drift depend on who meets which item: $v_{ij} = \theta_j - b_i$, the respondent's ability minus the item's difficulty. Put that into the accuracy formula:
$$\Pr(x_{ij} = 1) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\alpha(\theta_j - b_i)}}.$$
That is a 2PL whose slope is the boundary separation. [Tuerlinckx and De Boeck (2005)](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-000-0810-3) read discrimination this way: the amount of information collected before responding. Whose boundary is it? If it belongs to the item (a time limit, a format that invites care), $\alpha_i$ is an ordinary item slope. If it belongs to the respondent, a cautious respondent's answers discriminate more: a slope that varies by person, which the 2PL doesn't have. [Van der Maas et al. (2011)](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0022749) follow this line to a new model for accuracy and speed, and `diffIRT` fits diffusion item response models ([Molenaar, Tuerlinckx & van der Maas, 2015](https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v066.i04)).

#### Try it: the 2PL inside the walk

Drift is θ − b. Curves are the closed forms for two boundaries; dots are proportions correct in 200 simulated walks at each ability (first boundary).

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof ir_b = Inputs.range([-2, 2], {value: 0, step: 0.1, label: "Item difficulty b"})
viewof ir_a1 = Inputs.range([0.4, 3], {value: 1, step: 0.1, label: "First boundary α"})
viewof ir_a2 = Inputs.range([0.4, 3], {value: 2, step: 0.1, label: "Second boundary α"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
ir = diffusionIRT(ir_b, ir_a1, ir_a2)
ir_dom = [`boundary ${ir_a1.toFixed(1)}`, `boundary ${ir_a2.toFixed(1)}`]
html`<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:1rem">${
  Plot.plot({width: 340, height: 280, x: {label: "Ability θ"}, y: {label: "P(correct)", domain: [0, 1]},
    color: {legend: true, domain: ir_dom, range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
    marks: [Plot.ruleX([ir_b], {stroke: palette.rule}),
            Plot.line(ir.curves, {x: "theta", y: "p", stroke: "curve", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
            Plot.dot(ir.dots, {x: "theta", y: "p", fill: palette.ink, r: 3.5})]})
}${
  Plot.plot({width: 340, height: 280, x: {label: "Ability θ"}, y: {label: "Mean decision time (s)"},
    color: {domain: ir_dom, range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
    marks: [Plot.ruleX([ir_b], {stroke: palette.rule}),
            Plot.line(ir.curves, {x: "theta", y: "mdt", stroke: "curve", strokeWidth: 2.5})]})
}</div>`
```

The dots, from nothing but random walks, sit on a logistic curve, steeper for the wider boundary, as for a larger slope. The right panel is what a 2PL can't see: decisions take longest for respondents near the item's difficulty, where the drift is near zero, and the wider boundary takes longer everywhere. The model predicts which responses should be slow, not only which should be right.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "Two respondents have the same ability. One sets a boundary of 2, the other of 1. On an item with $b$ one logit below their ability, what are their chances of a correct response?",
  options: [
    {text: "About 0.88 for the cautious one and 0.73 for the other", correct: true, why: "The drift is $\\theta - b = 1$, so $1/(1 + e^{-2}) \\approx 0.88$ and $1/(1 + e^{-1}) \\approx 0.73$. The cautious respondent pays for the extra accuracy in time."},
    {text: "The same, 0.73, because they have the same ability", correct: false, why: "Under the diffusion model accuracy depends on the product $\\alpha v$, not on $v$ alone. Equal abilities give equal drifts, not equal accuracies."},
    {text: "About 0.98 and 0.73", correct: false, why: "$1/(1+e^{-4}) \\approx 0.98$ would need $\\alpha v = 4$. Here it is $2 \\times 1 = 2$."}
  ]
})
```

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Go deeper: from the random walk to the 2PL

**Assumptions:** a diffusion process with drift $v$ and noise SD $s$ per unit time, absorbing boundaries at 0 and $\alpha$, a start at $z$, and no across-trial variability. The process ends at one boundary or the other with probability 1.

Let $h(x)$ be the probability of reaching $\alpha$ before 0 from position $x$. Over a short step, $h(x) = \mathbb{E}\,h(x + v\,dt + s\,dW)$; expanding to second order gives $v\,h'(x) + \tfrac{s^2}{2} h''(x) = 0$, with $h(0) = 0$ and $h(\alpha) = 1$. The solution is
$$h(z) = \frac{1 - e^{-2vz/s^2}}{1 - e^{-2v\alpha/s^2}}.$$
At $z = \alpha/2$ the numerator is $1 - e^{-v\alpha/s^2}$ and the denominator factors as $(1 - e^{-v\alpha/s^2})(1 + e^{-v\alpha/s^2})$, so
$$h(\alpha/2) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\alpha v/s^2}}.$$
With $v = \theta_j - b_i$ this is a 2PL with slope $\alpha/s^2$ ([Tuerlinckx & De Boeck, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-000-0810-3)). Two assumptions did the work. A biased start ($z \ne \alpha/2$) moves the curve, so it no longer passes through 0.5 at $\theta = b$. Across-trial variability in drift averages logistic curves with different arguments, which flattens the curve. Either way the model is still an item response model, just not exactly a 2PL.
:::

### Slow errors

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [Item response data and the IRW](irw-data.qmd): in the brightness experiment of `rr98_accuracy`, errors were slower than correct responses (median 0.65 s against 0.56 s), but within a brightness level by only about 0.02 s. Most of the gap came from errors crowding into the hard levels, which are slow for every response.
:::

The simple model predicts no gap within a condition, as the first widget showed. Data can have one (the motion task below does), and [Ratcliff and Rouder (1998)](https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9280.00067) account for it with variability *across* trials. If the drift varies from trial to trial (SD $\eta$), errors come mostly from trials whose drift happened to be low, and those trials are slow however they end: errors are slower. If the starting point varies, errors come mostly from trials that began near the wrong boundary, and those end quickly: errors are faster ([Ratcliff & McKoon, 2008](https://doi.org/10.1162/neco.2008.12-06-420)).

The pooled `rr98_accuracy` gap is the same mechanism one level up, with conditions of different difficulty playing the part of trials with different drift. So before reading slow errors as drift variability, compare error and correct times *within* a condition, and within a respondent.

#### Try it: variability across trials

3,000 trials with drift 1.5 and boundary 2 on average.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof se_eta = Inputs.range([0, 2], {value: 0, step: 0.1, label: "Across-trial SD of drift (η)"})
viewof se_sz = Inputs.range([0, 0.9], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Across-trial range of the start (share of α)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
se = diffusionTrials(1.5, 2, 0.5, 0.3, se_eta, se_sz, 3000, 7, 0)
se_cb = bins(se.rows.filter(d => d.resp === 1).map(d => d.rt), 0, 3, 50)
se_eb = bins(se.rows.filter(d => d.resp === 0).map(d => d.rt), 0, 3, 50)
Plot.plot({width: 700, height: 240, x: {label: "Seconds", domain: [0, 3]}, y: {label: "Trials (errors below)"},
  marks: [Plot.rectY(se_cb, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: "count", fill: palette.main}),
          Plot.rectY(se_eb, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: d => -d.count, fill: palette.contrast}),
          Plot.ruleX([se.medCorrect], {stroke: palette.main, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
          Plot.ruleX([se.medError], {stroke: palette.contrast, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
          Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: palette.rule})]})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Accuracy **${se.acc.toFixed(2)}**. Median time: correct **${se.medCorrect.toFixed(2)} s**, errors **${se.medError.toFixed(2)} s** (a gap of ${(se.medError - se.medCorrect).toFixed(2)} s, from ${se.nError} errors).`
```

With both sliders at 0 the dashed medians nearly coincide (a gap of 0.03 s). Raise η to 1 and errors become slower by about a quarter of a second, and accuracy falls. Put η back to 0 and raise the start's range to 0.8: now errors are *faster*, by about as much.

### Race models for more than two options

One accumulator with two boundaries suits two responses. With three or more (the ink colours in a Stroop task, the options of a multiple-choice item) the natural extension is a **race**: each option has its own accumulator, and the first to reach its threshold gives the response and the time ([Heathcote & Matzke, 2022](https://doi.org/10.1177/09637214221095852)). [Tuerlinckx and De Boeck (2005)](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-000-0810-3) derive the 2PL from a race model too. The **linear ballistic accumulator** ([Brown & Heathcote, 2008](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cogpsych.2007.12.002)) is the tractable case: independent accumulators rise in straight lines, without noise within a trial, toward a common threshold, which gives closed-form solutions for any number of options. Problem 6 sketches one.

## Simulate

The code generates 100 respondents × 200 trials as plain random walks in base R (a second or two), each respondent with their own drift, boundary and non-decision time. It recovers them with EZ-diffusion and compares an accuracy-only ability, the logit of the proportion correct, with the truth.

With the seed as written, EZ's average drift matches the truth (1.47), and its estimates correlate 0.97 with the true drifts, 0.97 with the boundaries and 0.93 with the non-decision times (boundaries run slightly wide, 1.32 against 1.29, from the walk's step size). The accuracy-only ability correlates 0.86 with drift and 0.47 with the boundary: judged by accuracy alone, a careful respondent looks abler. Now set `eta <- 1`. EZ's average drift falls to 1.18 against a true 1.47, though it still orders respondents well (0.95), and errors become slower than correct responses (0.67 against 0.64 s).


::: {.cell}
```{webr}
#| results: markup
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| include: true
#| label: unnamed-chunk-17
# Simulate two-choice decisions from the diffusion model and recover its parameters
# with EZ-diffusion (Wagenmakers, van der Maas & Grasman, 2007).
#
# Each respondent j has a drift theta_j (their ability: the drift on an item of
# difficulty 0), a boundary separation alpha_j (caution) and a non-decision time ter_j.
# Every trial is a random walk that starts halfway between 0 and alpha_j and moves by
# v * dt plus Gaussian noise with SD sqrt(dt) (so s = 1) until it crosses a boundary.
# eta is the across-trial SD of drift: 0 is the simple model; try eta <- 1.
#
# Base R only. The walk takes a second or two in the browser; raise np and ntr as
# you like in your own R session. (rtdists::rdiffusion samples the same model
# without the small error from the size of the steps.)
set.seed(65)
np  <- 100     # respondents
ntr <- 200     # trials per respondent
eta <- 0       # across-trial SD of drift

theta <- rnorm(np, 1.5, 0.5)       # drift
bound <- runif(np, 0.8, 1.8)       # boundary separation
ter   <- runif(np, 0.3, 0.5)       # non-decision time (s)

walk <- function(v, a, ter, dt = 0.001, maxt = 10) {
  n <- length(v); x <- a / 2; t <- numeric(n); resp <- rep(NA_integer_, n)
  live <- seq_len(n)
  while (length(live) && t[live[1]] < maxt) {
    x[live] <- x[live] + v[live] * dt + sqrt(dt) * rnorm(length(live))
    t[live] <- t[live] + dt
    up <- x[live] >= a[live]; lo <- x[live] <= 0
    resp[live[up]] <- 1L; resp[live[lo]] <- 0L
    live <- live[!(up | lo)]
  }
  data.frame(rt = ter + t, resp = resp)
}

d <- data.frame(id = rep(1:np, each = ntr))
d$v <- theta[d$id] + eta * rnorm(nrow(d))      # this trial's drift
d <- cbind(d, walk(d$v, bound[d$id], ter[d$id]))

# EZ-diffusion from the proportion correct and the mean and variance of correct RTs.
ez <- function(pc, vrt, mrt, s = 1) {
  L <- qlogis(pc)
  x <- L * (L * pc^2 - L * pc + pc - 0.5) / vrt
  v <- sign(pc - 0.5) * s * x^(1/4)
  a <- s^2 * L / v
  y <- -v * a / s^2
  mdt <- (a / (2 * v)) * (1 - exp(y)) / (1 + exp(y))
  c(v = v, alpha = a, ter = mrt - mdt)
}
est <- as.data.frame(t(sapply(split(d, d$id), function(x) {
  pc <- mean(x$resp); if (pc == 1) pc <- 1 - 1 / (2 * nrow(x))
  rc <- x$rt[x$resp == 1]
  c(pc = pc, ez(pc, var(rc), mean(rc)))
})))

# Everyone takes the same trials, so the sum score is sufficient and the logit of the
# proportion correct orders respondents as a Rasch ability estimate would.
acc_ability <- qlogis(est$pc)

round(c(accuracy = mean(d$resp),
        median_correct = median(d$rt[d$resp == 1]), median_error = median(d$rt[d$resp == 0])), 2)
round(rbind(truth = c(v = mean(theta), alpha = mean(bound), ter = mean(ter)),
            EZ = colMeans(est[, c("v", "alpha", "ter")]),
            r = c(cor(est$v, theta), cor(est$alpha, bound), cor(est$ter, ter))), 2)
round(c(r_accuracy_drift = cor(acc_ability, theta), r_accuracy_boundary = cor(acc_ability, bound)), 2)

op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 1, 1))
plot(theta, est$v, pch = 19, col = "#2780e3", xlab = "True drift", ylab = "EZ drift")
abline(0, 1, lty = 2, col = "#999999")
plot(bound, acc_ability, pch = 19, col = "#c2410c",
     xlab = "True boundary separation", ylab = "Logit of proportion correct")
par(op)

```
