Ability tracks drift ($r = 0.82$), and the boundary as well ($r = 0.50$): a respondent who waits for more evidence gets more right, and a model of accuracy alone credits that to ability. Speed from the lognormal model is mostly the boundary ($r = -0.71$) and the non-decision time ($r = -0.77$), and barely relates to drift ($r = 0.19$). Ability and speed are nearly unrelated ($r = -0.19$), much as in the ROAR lexical task of the response-time lesson. The diffusion model reads that near-zero as speed and ability being built from different parts of the process.

### Random-dot motion: the model's story, and slow errors

In [O'Brien and Yeatman's (2021)](https://doi.org/10.1111/desc.13039) study of dyslexia, 106 school-aged children reported the direction of a field of moving dots, of which 6%, 12%, 24%, 48% or 100% moved together (the coherence). An IRW item is a block of the session and a coherence level, met 10 times, so each child has 60 trials per level ([processing script](https://github.com/ben-domingue/irw/blob/main/data/motion_discrimination.R)). Keying: 1 is correct.


